12
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.170
I am in the fancy New York offices of Stitcher.

13
00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.390
Get out!

14
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.930
You're in New York City!

15
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:41.690
Yeah!

16
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.590
And guess what, lady?

17
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:44.390
What?

18
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.170
Guess what?

19
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:46.010
What?

20
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.210
Our picture is on the wall here.

21
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:49.450
In New York?

22
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:50.050
Yes!

23
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.370
In the New York offices!

24
00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.690
They have our Office Ladies picture on the wall.

25
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:54.930
I took a picture with it.

26
00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:55.650
Ah!

27
00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:56.730
That's so cool!

28
00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:57.290
I know.

29
00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.870
We have photos in two cities.

30
00:00:59.010 --> 00:00:59.710
In two cities!

31
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:00.770
That's the big time!

32
00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.110
We're bi-coastal!

33
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:03.710
We are!

34
00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:04.110
Right now!

35
00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:04.790
Wait a second!

36
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:06.170
Right now we're bi-coastal.

37
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.130
Office Ladies has gone bi-coastal.

38
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.690
That feels real boss, lady.

39
00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:10.450
It does.

40
00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.710
I like it.

41
00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:12.270
Me too.

42
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:17.110
Well, since we're talking about being in offices, Jenna, guess who's back at Dunder Mifflin?

43
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.070
Toby.

44
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.790
Well, not everybody's happy about it.

45
00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:21.390
No!

46
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:22.370
God!

47
00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.230
No!

48
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:24.890
God!

49
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:25.850
Please, no!

50
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:26.490
No!

51
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:27.570
No!

52
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.160
That's right.

53
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.620
Today, we are talking about Frame Toby!

54
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.440
It is Season 5, Episode 9.

55
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.200
It is written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Jason Reitman.

56
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.460
That's a good duo.

57
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.160
I'm going to give you a summary.

58
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.420
I was hoping you would.

59
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.620
Michael reacts poorly to the revelation that Toby is back at the Scranton Branch.

60
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.220
I like the use of the word poorly.

61
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.440
I know.

62
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:55.980
That's an understatement.

63
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:02.780
So, he and Dwight hatch a plan to frame Toby for drugs that Michael has purchased from

64
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.940
the two Vance Refrigeration employees, Gino and Leo.

65
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:13.720
Pam is incensed by a mess that is left in the microwave by one of her co-workers, we don't

66
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.200
know who.

67
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.940
She leaves a pointed note, which elicits mixed reactions from the fellow employees.

68
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.640
Meanwhile, Ryan breaks up with Kelly, and Jim surprises Pam with the news that he bought

69
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.120
his parents' house for them.

70
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.380
That's a lot.

71
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.220
It's a lot.

72
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.260
These headphones are tight.

73
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:34.760
Mm-hmm.

74
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.220
The headphones are tight in New York.

75
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.060
I fixed them.

76
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.440
Do you want a different pair?

77
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.020
No, I fixed them.

78
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:41.160
Okay.

79
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.720
You guys, that was Jared in New York.

80
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:44.380
Yeah.

81
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.880
Jared is the Sam of New York.

82
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:47.160
Yeah.

83
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.100
Sam, say hi.

84
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.340
A little dig at LA there in our loose headphones.

85
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:51.340
Why?

86
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.640
I think she likes some loose.

87
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:53.940
I like a loose headphone.

88
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.100
Oh, in that case, take that, Jared.

89
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.040
Jared's all squeezing my brain with the headphones here in New York.

90
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.540
Should we go into Fast Facts?

91
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:04.520
I think we should.

92
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.280
All right.

93
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.720
Fast fact number one.

94
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.060
You might remember, because we shared this earlier, that NBC ordered 28 episodes for

95
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:12.700
season five.

96
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.140
It was our longest season.

97
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.360
I remember, Jenna, when we wrapped season five, we all got sick.

98
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.560
Oh, yeah.

99
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.540
Like, we all got the flu or something.

100
00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.860
Because I remember the next week, like, texting a few people.

101
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.780
I'm like, I have a cold.

102
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.040
And Kate was like, yeah, me too.

103
00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.460
Like, I think our bodies were just like.

104
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:29.460
Yeah.

105
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.840
A standard network television season usually runs on television from September to May.

106
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.700
We work from July to March, right?

107
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.620
So that we can get the episodes ready.

108
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.940
And a standard television schedule is usually where you do three weeks on, one week off

109
00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:49.840
of filming.

110
00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.840
So usually a show will take one hiatus week a month.

111
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.760
But the writers always continue to work for that week.

112
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.900
That week off is meant so that the writers and the producer, they have a sort of catch-up week.

113
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.260
Because they're constantly writing, prepping, editing, delivering the episodes.

114
00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.380
They would have these episodes in multiple forMs.

115
00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.720
They're, like, doing an outline for one, but they're writing another.

116
00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.180
They're punching up another one.

117
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.180
They're doing post-editing on another one.

118
00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.340
They've got, like, five episodes going at once.

119
00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.080
We would just, like, be like, what are we filming this week?

120
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:31.040
This year, with this crazy 28-episode season, Steve Carell also had a commitment to do a film.

121
00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.660
I think it was Get Smart.

122
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.900
And he needed, like, a six- to eight-week break from the show in the middle of that July-to-March

123
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.080
filming schedule so he could film Get Smart.

124
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.680
How did we ever do that?

125
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.980
In order to do that, we had to film 19 episodes in a row.

126
00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.980
That's when we got sick.

127
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.900
That's when we got sick.

128
00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.700
I just have a memory of a bunch of us getting a cold bug.

129
00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.320
That was it.

130
00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:57.960
Yeah.

131
00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.800
So Frame Toby was right in the middle of this crazy 19-episode schedule.

132
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:04.840
Oy, oy, oy.

133
00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.040
And here's why I think this is significant.

134
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:15.840
It's really crazy to me that they decided to bring Toby back in the middle of this 19-episode stretch.

135
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.440
Because remember, he was now our showrunner because Greg was doing Parks and Rec.

136
00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.200
So Paul Lieberstein is now showrunning and acting in an episode.

137
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.540
Again, because they gave him that break.

138
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.460
And now they're like, sorry, bud.

139
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.040
You need to be Toby again.

140
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.180
You're back in the mix.

141
00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:35.260
So I reached out to Jen Salata, who was running the show with him.

142
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.360
And I said, do you know why you did that?

143
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.620
Did you just want Paul to never sleep?

144
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.040
What was your plan there?

145
00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:47.720
And she said she can't remember exactly the reasoning, but because Holly had left,

146
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.660
they were either going to have to cast someone new or bring Toby back.

147
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.120
And that dynamic between Toby and Michael was so beloved.

148
00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:00.180
She said as a fan of the show, she wanted Toby to come back to that role.

149
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:06.340
As a co-showrunner, she was freaked out at the idea of Paul having to wear both hats.

150
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.300
But she said he did it so well.

151
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.580
And Paul said he doesn't remember sleeping a lot.

152
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:12.340
Aw.

153
00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.200
But also, he enjoyed doing it.

154
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.380
You know, he liked his time on set with us.

155
00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.260
Well, I love Toby coming back just because watching Michael spiral is delicious.

156
00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.200
This is one of my favorite episodes of the season for that reason.

157
00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.740
So thank you, Paul, for doing that.

158
00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:30.660
But thank you.

159
00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.300
My fast fact number two is a big thank you to James Carey,

160
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.580
because he gave us the shooting draft for Frame Toby.

161
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:41.400
Yes, James.

162
00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.500
This was so fun to watch it with the shooting draft.

163
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.600
Jenna and I were texting each other and calling each other like,

164
00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:48.500
oh my God, that's not in.

165
00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:49.160
Oh, but this is.

166
00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.000
But did you see that?

167
00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.100
It was really fun.

168
00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.560
And this is a particularly amazing episode to have the script for,

169
00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.980
because this microwave runner, the dirty microwave runner,

170
00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.820
it was much bigger in the original script.

171
00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:01.740
Yes.

172
00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.080
And it was full of Angela and Pam stuff.

173
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:05.860
Yes.

174
00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.040
So we get to share about all that today.

175
00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.040
So thank you, James Carey.

176
00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.280
Thank you, James Carey.

177
00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:15.080
We are also going to have some great location details.

178
00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.680
Thanks to Randy Cordray.

179
00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.760
He told me all about Jim and Pam's new house.

180
00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.500
And I have to share something that Randy said in his email to me

181
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.560
when I reached out to him about this episode.

182
00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:28.480
Are you ready?

183
00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.640
He said, a particular delight of looking forward to your questions each week

184
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.920
is that I get to revisit each episode to refresh my memory

185
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.780
of what we collectively accomplished.

186
00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.120
Here's what his process is when I write him an email.

187
00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:42.720
Oh.

188
00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:47.260
He says, my process is to reread the shooting draft,

189
00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.980
watch the episode, watch any deleted scenes,

190
00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.880
and listen to any commentary that might have been recorded.

191
00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.340
Randy, this is what I do every week.

192
00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:57.960
This is what Jenna and I are doing.

193
00:07:58.620 --> 00:07:59.660
That's what I said.

194
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.060
I said, you are an office lady.

195
00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.580
You are an office lady.

196
00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.900
And Jenna, every time you email him a question now,

197
00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:07.120
I'm going to be like, don't ask him that.

198
00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.280
I don't want to send him down a rabbit hole.

199
00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:10.060
I know.

200
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.540
But he really said he's really been liking it.

201
00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.300
And remember when our podcast for weight loss came out

202
00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.080
and Randy wrote us that nice email?

203
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.700
He wrote us the most lovely email.

204
00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:19.300
Yeah.

205
00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.000
He was just like, ladies, you nailed it.

206
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.760
You got every bit of information that could be known

207
00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.100
about how we did that engagement scene

208
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.740
with the rain and the parking lot.

209
00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:29.960
I'm so proud of you, too.

210
00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:30.720
I know.

211
00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:32.500
It was like we got an email from our dad.

212
00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:33.480
That's how I felt.

213
00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:34.020
Me, too.

214
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.260
Like, dad thought we did a good job.

215
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:35.800
I know.

216
00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.300
I want Randy's respect.

217
00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.080
I do, too, because I respect him.

218
00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.420
Look what he's doing just off an email of questions.

219
00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:42.980
We got to have him on the show.

220
00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:43.960
He's got to come on.

221
00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:44.980
He's an office lady.

222
00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:48.040
We need to have Randy and James come on.

223
00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:48.860
Yes.

224
00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:50.180
Because they worked together.

225
00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.580
OK, Future Revisited, everybody.

226
00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.240
Did you hear that, Randy and James?

227
00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.740
I guess that's my serious tone.

228
00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:56.380
What am I doing?

229
00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:57.320
Did you hear that?

230
00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.100
You're coming on the pod, fellas.

231
00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.380
I'm going to move us into fast fact number three.

232
00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:02.360
OK.

233
00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.900
I think the world is going to be grateful for this fast fact

234
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.520
because I was grateful for it.

235
00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:09.880
Oh, no.

236
00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.980
Somebody did a deep dive.

237
00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.360
This is how to clean a microwave.

238
00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:15.560
Oh, Lord.

239
00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.420
This episode's about dirty microwaves.

240
00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.520
And I thought, how could we clean this microwave?

241
00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.200
This episode is about dirty microwaves.

242
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.740
All right.

243
00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.620
Ainsley Bubico, our associate producer, helped me with this.

244
00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.260
And I love it.

245
00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.900
Here is how to clean a real dirty microwave.

246
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:34.780
OK.

247
00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:35.540
All right.

248
00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.660
Get a microwave-safe bowl.

249
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.000
Fill it with two cups of water and two tablespoons of vinegar.

250
00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:49.180
Now, side note, if you'd like, you could add a drop of your favorite essential oil if you want.

251
00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:50.480
If you want to be fancy.

252
00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:51.000
Yeah.

253
00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.460
You can clean your microwave fancy or regular.

254
00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:54.380
OK.

255
00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.460
Now, turn the microwave on.

256
00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.340
Obviously, put the bowl in the microwave.

257
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.600
And then put it on high for five minutes.

258
00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.720
You want it to get real steamy in there.

259
00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:03.120
OK.

260
00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.280
That's what she said.

261
00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:05.000
Mm-hmm.

262
00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.520
After it's done, don't open the microwave door for 15 minutes.

263
00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:10.420
Why?

264
00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:12.080
Because you'll burn yourself or something?

265
00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:16.500
No, because that makes all the baked-on grime sort of soften.

266
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:16.850
